<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zakar-Baal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 1075 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> · Also known as Tjekerbaal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zakar-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a> was a king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> around 1075 BCE, known from the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/story-of-wenamun">Story of Wenamun</a>. When the envoy Wenamun arrived to obtain cedar for the barque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>, Zakar-Baal demanded payment, consulted his ancestors' records of earlier transactions and treated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as a trading partner, not an overlord. The episode illustrates the decline of Egyptian power in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zakar-baal.jpg" alt="Zakar-Baal" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ancient Egyptian Writer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wenamun-papyrus.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
